{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR DECISION 2026-08-05: REMOVE the field\n\nThe operator ruled: remove `CodeHealthConfig.enabled`, consistent with the dce2 ruling on the\ninert `analyzers` field.\n\nI raised the counter-argument when presenting the choice — that this differs from dce2 because\ndeleting `analyzers` removed a knob that never did anything, whereas deleting `enabled` removes\nthe only advertised OFF SWITCH for the whole code-health subsystem. The operator considered it\nand chose removal. Recording that the tradeoff was surfaced and knowingly accepted.\n\nScope: remove the field, its coercion row, and its config-surface entries (including the\n`REBAR_CODE_HEALTH_ENABLED` env var), and ensure the docs no longer advertise an off switch\nthat does not exist.\n\nFollow dce2's landed precedent for the mechanics — it is the template: dce2 also had to add a\nwarn-class TOMBSTONE for the removed key, because `test_config_surface_golden.py` fails the\nbuild when a golden-pinned key exists in neither the live schema nor the tombstone registry. A\nstill-set key must WARN and exit 0, not be silently dropped. Expect the same requirement here,\nand check whether removing this field orphans a coercion helper the way dce2 orphaned\n`_as_str_dict`.\n\nThe guard AC (preventing silent regression to inert) is moot for a deleted field, but note it\ndepends on bug 2c58-e710-275e-4ff7 anyway — the current gate could never have caught this\nfield, because `.enabled` is read on other configs."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785911350175714001,"uuid":"5a31fc63-d0f3-4dc9-97a7-99d2318675fb"}